    "stdout_excerpt": "COUNTERFACT_JSON={\"expected\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"YfinanceProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"YFINANCE_METRICS_SHAPE_INVALID\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"observed\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"YfinanceProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"YFINANCE_METRICS_SHAPE_INVALID\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"reason\": \"a real upstream defect -- the fundamentals-timeseries endpoint answering with an empty result array, which is what Yahoo returns while a symbol's measure series is being rebuilt -- leaves this row with no trailing point to label Current and no dated column to emit, so the fetch returns None and the row mapper refuses with YfinanceProviderError/YFINANCE_METRICS_SHAPE_INVALID rather than publish a metrics row whose values were never served. This entry replaces the wave-32/33 claim that the row was blocked by an upstream SDK capability gap: that was measured against a transport targeting Ticker.get_valuation_measures (the 1.7.0 generation's method, indeed absent here), while the same nine measures are served by the fundamentals-timeseries endpoint the statements already use, which the pinned 0.2.66 can request through its own data layer. Measured on this row's own document: control leg emits 2 rows (Current + one dated period), tampered leg refuses.; untampered control produced 2 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
  },
